1|0@NoStupidQuestions^1440398126#cudnq22:XeverSeven$:'Tea time' is a very British idea. Colonial/Post-Colonial America dropped a lot of customs from the motherland that were formal as we tended towards efficiency in everything. By the industrial revolution, we had replaced tea with coffee, and decided it wasn't just for the upper classes.
1|0@NoStupidQuestions^1440355754#cud1h6c:MrFalconGarcia$:The American Revolution.
1|1@NoStupidQuestions^1440350707#cucyo7y:AtlasDM$:Tea just doesn't convey the same cultural ideal that black coffee does. Stereotypical images of cowboys/soldiers/blue-collar workers include coffee and suggest that the drinker is a hardworking American that can do anything as long as he has a hot cup of coffee. I'm sure part of the popularity is a lasting result of advertising just as much as a result of past political affairs. Side note: Can you imagine a WWII poster of a GI drinking a soy mocha latte with foam? No thanks!
